<figure id="A1.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Ablation for N on Different Datasets. To systematically investigate the impact of batch size on performance, we conduct ablation experiments with varying batch sizes (<math id="A1.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1k,2k,5k,10k,15k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1k,2k,5k,10k,15k</annotation></semantics></math>). We evaluate how these changes affect <span id="A1.T4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Modest-Align</span>’s performance, especially on text-to-image and image retrieval metrics, to better understand the role of batch size in embedding smoothing. Our results indicate that a batch size of 10k offers optimal performance.</figcaption>
